@startuml redis-01-topology
!include _style.iuml
title @RedisSentinel(replicas = 2, sentinels = 3) — six containers, one private network

rectangle "Docker bridge network  ·  created per test class  ·  destroyed with it" as NET {

  rectangle "sentinel1\n:26379" as S1
  rectangle "sentinel2\n:26379" as S2
  rectangle "sentinel3\n:26379" as S3

  rectangle "**redis-master**\n:6379  ·  accepts writes" as M #1c2a1c

  rectangle "redis-replica1\n:6379  ·  read-only" as R1
  rectangle "redis-replica2\n:6379  ·  read-only" as R2

  S1 -[hidden]right- S2
  S2 -[hidden]right- S3
  R1 -[hidden]right- R2

  S1 -[#58a6ff]down-> M
  S2 -[#58a6ff]down-> M : monitor mymaster **by internal IP**
  S3 -[#58a6ff]down-> M

  M -[#9ece6a]down-> R1 : async replication
  M -[#9ece6a]down-> R2

  S1 -[#7d8590,dashed]right- S2 : <i>__sentinel__:hello</i>
  S2 -[#7d8590,dashed]right- S3 : <i>discovered, not configured</i>
}

note right of NET #12305a
  Every container also publishes its port
  to a **random host port**, so your test
  reaches any node directly.

  Sentinel, however, gossips and returns
  the **internal** address — which is why
  this topology is Linux-host / dev-container
  only. See FIG-05.
end note

legend left
  1 master + 2 replicas + 3 sentinels = 6 containers   |   quorum = (sentinels / 2) + 1 = **2**
  down-after-milliseconds = **2000 ms** (Redis default 30000)  ·  failover-timeout = **5000 ms** (default 180000)
endlegend
@enduml
